---
layout: base.njk
permalink: /contact/
title: Contact
description: "A question about Prospektor, or something you need from us? Send it here and we reply by email. No sales call, and no demo to sit through."
---

{#- #456, 31 Aug 2026. Operator: *"spawn a contact form thread. that form will
    be reachable both via the main nav and footer. I enabled contact forms in
    netlify"*.

    THE ONE RULE THIS PAGE MUST NOT BREAK: it is not a sales-call door.
    `llms.txt` says *"No sales call. The price is on the page and checkout is
    the next click"*, and that was deliberate — a contact form is the obvious
    way to reintroduce a qualification call by the back door. So the lede says
    the opposite out loud, there is no "book a demo", no company-size field and
    no phone number. Somebody who wants to buy is one click from Stripe and
    this page tells them so.

    Three fields, and that is the whole argument for the design (the style
    skill's rule 8, one thing at a time). A support question and a partnership
    enquiry want different fields; asking for the union of them gets neither,
    so the message box does that work and a person reads it.

    Netlify Forms handles the POST — the operator enabled it, and the site
    already registers `founding-spot` the same way from /checkout/. Honeypot,
    not reCAPTCHA (#456): reCAPTCHA puts an active Google script on the page,
    which lands on consent.js's gate, on privacy §08's table and on §12's
    transfer paragraph — three documents and a consent decision, to stop spam
    a form with no traffic yet is not getting. If it ever needs one, that is
    its own row. -#}

<script type="application/ld+json">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "ContactPage",
  "name": "Contact Prospektor",
  "url": "{{ site.url }}/contact/",
  "description": {{ description | dump | safe }},
  "isPartOf": { "@id": "{{ site.url }}/#website" },
  "about": { "@id": "{{ site.url }}/#organization" },
  "mainEntity": {
    "@type": "ContactPoint",
    "contactType": "customer support",
    "email": "{{ site.email }}",
    "url": "{{ site.url }}/contact/",
    "availableLanguage": "English"
  }
}
</script>

<header class="page-hero">
  <div class="page-hero-inner">
    <span class="tag">contact</span>
    <h1 class="page-h1">Ask us anything.</h1>
    <p class="page-lede">
      This goes to our inbox and we reply by email. You don't need to talk to
      anyone to buy — the price is on <a href="/pricing/">the pricing page</a>
      and checkout is the next click.
    </p>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="section contact-section">
  <div class="contact-inner">

    {#- action= is a page this build really writes (src/contact-thanks.njk).
        netlify.toml ends in a /* → /404.html catch-all and Netlify handles form
        POSTs ahead of redirects, but the success URL is resolved as an ordinary
        request afterwards — so a thanks page that did not build would 404 the
        person who just wrote to us. test/contact.test.js asserts the file
        exists rather than trusting it. -#}
    <form class="contact-form" name="contact" method="POST" action="/contact/thanks/"
          netlify netlify-honeypot="bot-field">
      {#- Netlify needs the form's name in the POST body itself; without this
          the submission is accepted and filed under nothing. -#}
      <input type="hidden" name="form-name" value="contact">

      <div class="contact-row">
        <label class="contact-label" for="contactName">Your name</label>
        <input class="contact-input" id="contactName" name="name" type="text"
               autocomplete="name" required>
      </div>

      <div class="contact-row">
        <label class="contact-label" for="contactEmail">Your email</label>
        <input class="contact-input" id="contactEmail" name="email" type="email"
               autocomplete="email" placeholder="you@yourcompany.com" required>
      </div>

      <div class="contact-row">
        <label class="contact-label" for="contactMessage">Your message</label>
        <textarea class="contact-input contact-textarea" id="contactMessage"
                  name="message" rows="6" required></textarea>
      </div>

      {#- The honeypot. Off-screen rather than display:none, and aria-hidden, so
          a screen reader never offers it and a bot filling every field trips
          it. Same idiom as the reserve form on /checkout/. -#}
      <input type="text" name="bot-field" tabindex="-1" autocomplete="off"
             style="position:absolute;left:-9999px;" aria-hidden="true">

      <button class="btn-cta contact-submit" type="submit">Send it</button>
    </form>

    <p class="contact-note">
      Your name, address and message reach us through Netlify's form service and
      are read to answer you — nothing else, and no mailing list, because we
      don't run one. <a href="/privacy/#applicants">What we do with it, in full</a>.
      You can also just write to
      <a href="mailto:{{ site.email }}">{{ site.email }}</a>.
    </p>

  </div>
</section>
